# What may enter a Docker build context. Allowlist, not blocklist.
#
# The signer image is the one artifact in this repo that runs inside a TEE, and
# the whole claim about it is that nobody can extract a key from it. A build
# context assembled by exclusion gets that wrong the first time someone adds a
# file: `.env`, `.keys/`, and every `*.bak` in the tree would have been in scope
# by default. Starting from `*` means a new secret is out unless someone
# deliberately lets it in.
#
# It also fixes a real break. Without this file the runtime stage copied the
# host's `node_modules` over the one `pnpm install` had just built, and on
# Windows those are symlinks to `D:/CODE/...` — paths with no meaning in a Linux
# container. The image built cleanly and then died at boot on
# `Cannot find package '@ntux402/shared'`.

# Ignore everything...
*

# ...then re-admit exactly what services/signer/Dockerfile copies.
!pnpm-lock.yaml
!pnpm-workspace.yaml
!package.json
!tsconfig.base.json
!packages/shared
!services/signer

# Build output and installed trees are produced inside the image, never carried
# into it. `dist/` is gitignored, so a fresh clone has none and relying on the
# host's copy makes the image depend on whoever built it last.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/.vite
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Nothing below is needed to run the signer, and none of it should be within
# reach of a COPY in an enclave image.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.bak
**/.git

# Payer key material, in *both* spellings.
#
# `!services/signer` above re-admits that whole directory, so the key store is
# back in scope unless it is named here — and .gitignore already records what a
# single missing spelling costs: it carried `keys/` alone for a while, and "a
# dot's difference was enough to leave real payer private keys stageable".
# The same allowlist, the same directory, the same mistake available twice.
**/.keys
**/keys

# `oasis rofl build` output. 84 MB, and it is the *previous* enclave bundle —
# building an image that contains the last image is circular as well as large.
# It postdates the Dockerfile, so the first build never saw one and nothing
# caught it; .gitignore excludes `*.orc` for the same reason and this is the
# missing half of that rule.
**/*.orc
